#Problem APEX 3.5.29

## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Applications of differentiation)
## DBsection(Summary of curve sketching)
## KEYWORDS('implicit differentiation', 'increasing', 'decreasing', 'concavity')
## Institution(Valdosta State University)
## Author(S. V. Ault)
## Level(4)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('APEX Calculus')
## AuthorText1('Hartman')
## EditionText1('3.0')
## Section1('3.5')
## Problem1('28')

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl',         'PGML.pl','parserPopUp.pl',    'PGcourse.pl'
);
Context('Numeric')->variables->add(y => 'Real');

$r     = random(1, 5, 1);
$r2    = $r**2;
$f_eqn = "x^2 + y^2 = $r2";

$dydx = Compute('-x/y');
$d2ydx2 = Compute("-$r2/y^3");

$sign_opts = ['negative', 'positive', 'changes sign'];
$df_opts = ['increasing','decreasing', 'neither'];
$d2f_opts = ['concave up', 'concave down', 'neither'];

$q1df = DropDown($sign_opts, 0);
$q1fslope = DropDown($df_opts, 1);
$q1d2f = DropDown($sign_opts,0);
$q1concavity = DropDown($d2f_opts, 1);

$q2df = DropDown($sign_opts, 1);
$q2fslope = DropDown($df_opts, 0);
$q2d2f = DropDown($sign_opts,0);
$q2concavity = DropDown($d2f_opts, 1);

$q3df = DropDown($sign_opts, 0);
$q3fslope = DropDown($df_opts, 1);
$q3d2f = DropDown($sign_opts,1);
$q3concavity = DropDown($d2f_opts, 0);

$q4df = DropDown($sign_opts, 1);
$q4fslope = DropDown($df_opts, 0);
$q4d2f = DropDown($sign_opts,1);
$q4concavity = DropDown($d2f_opts, 0);



BEGIN_PGML
Given [`[$f_eqn]`], use implicit differentiation to find

a) [`` \frac{dy}{dx} = ``] [_]{$dydx}

b) [`` \frac{d^2y}{dx^2} = ``] [_]{$d2ydx2}

c) Based on your answers it is possible to determine the behavior
of the graph in each of the four quadrants.

    - *In Quadrant I:*

        [`f'`] is [_]{$q1df}{15}, so [`f`] is [_]{$q1fslope}{15}.  
        [`f''`] is [_]{$q1d2f}{15}, so [`f`] is [_]{$q1concavity}{15}.  

    - *In Quadrant II:*
    
        [`f'`] is [_]{$q2df}{15}, so [`f`] is [_]{$q2fslope}{15}.  
        [`f''`] is [_]{$q2d2f}{15}, so [`f`] is [_]{$q2concavity}{15}.  

    - *In Quadrant III:*
    
        [`f'`] is [_]{$q3df}{15}, so [`f`] is [_]{$q3fslope}{15}.  
        [`f''`] is [_]{$q3d2f}{15}, so [`f`] is [_]{$q3concavity}{15}.  

    - *In Quadrant IV,:*
    
        [`f'`] is [_]{$q4df}{15}, so [`f`] is [_]{$q4fslope}{15}.  
        [`f''`] is [_]{$q4d2f}{15}, so [`f`] is [_]{$q4concavity}{15}.  

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
First derivative:

[```
  \begin{aligned}
    x^2 + y^2 &= [$r2] \\[4pt]
    \dfrac{d}{dx}\left[ x^2 + y^2 \right] &= \dfrac{d}{dx}[ [$r2] ]\\[4pt]
    2x + 2y \dfrac{dy}{dx} &= 0\\[4pt]
    \dfrac{dy}{dx} &= [$dydx]
  \end{aligned}
```]

Second derivative:

[```
  \begin{aligned}
    \dfrac{d}{dx}\left[ [$dydx] \right] &= 
    -\dfrac{y - x \frac{dy}{dx}}{y^2}\\[4pt]
    &= -\dfrac{y - x \left( [$dydx] \right)}{y^2}\\[4pt]
    &= -\dfrac{y^2 + x^2}{y^3}\\[4pt]
    \dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2} &= [$d2ydx2]
  \end{aligned}
```]


(The last step made use of the original equation [`[$f_eqn]`].)

Check the values of [`f'`] and [`f''`] at sample points in 
each quadrant to determine increase, decrease, and concavity.

END_PGML_SOLUTION
ENDDOCUMENT();

